      "modelAnswer": "Holding a lock for an entire human-paced task is like reserving a meeting room for the whole afternoon just in case someone might want to glance at their notes for five minutes of it — everyone else who needs that room has to wait for the whole afternoon, not just the five minutes actually used.\n\n• The real cost: holding the lock for the whole \"open record → edit → save\" session means any other session that needs to touch that same record has to wait, and because a clinician's think time is unpredictable and can be long, that wait becomes real waiting time for someone else, not just a brief technical delay.\n\n• A practical fix: shrink the lock's scope and duration — for example, only acquire the lock immediately before the write itself, rather than holding it for the whole time the clinician is reading and editing on screen — or lock at a finer granularity, such as per patient record rather than a whole table, so unrelated records stay fully available to other clinicians.\n\n• A common mistake is: pessimistic locking's cost isn't about storage, raw database slowness, or SQL injection exposure — the real, well-documented cost is reduced concurrency from how long the lock is held, and the fix is to shrink that duration or granularity, not to raise isolation levels, coarsen locking, or resize a connection pool.\n\nSo the answer is: holding the lock for the whole session blocks other clinicians for as long as the first clinician's think time lasts; the fix is to shrink the lock's scope and duration, for example locking only right before the write."
