      "prompt": "Q11(a)\n\nAdmitting patient Maria Silva at St Clement's Hospital requires the Admissions coordinator service to update two independent systems: the Bed-Management system (allocate bed 12B) and the Billing system (open a new billing account). A Transaction Manager coordinates the two Resource Managers using Two-Phase Commit (2PC). Which statement correctly describes what happens in Phase 1 (Prepare) and Phase 2 (Commit), including specifically what each Resource Manager does — and does NOT do — during Phase 1?",
      "modelAnswer": "2PC is like a wedding where every guest must RSVP yes before the caterer is allowed to cook anything — nobody starts cooking just because one guest said yes.\n\n• Phase 1 (Prepare): the Transaction Manager asks both Resource Managers to prepare the admission. Each one works out and durably records its own intended change — Bed-Management notes \"intend to allocate bed 12B\", Billing notes \"intend to open a new billing account\" — and replies with a vote of yes or no.\n\n• What Phase 1 does NOT do: neither Resource Manager actually applies its change yet — the bed is not yet marked occupied, and the billing account is not yet opened. Phase 1 only produces a reversible, logged promise.\n\n• Phase 2 (Commit): only if both Resource Managers vote yes does the Transaction Manager move to Commit, instructing both to actually apply their previously-prepared change and acknowledge completion.\n\n• A common mistake is: applying the change immediately in Phase 1 and only reporting it afterward skips the whole point of preparing before committing; skipping Billing's vote entirely ignores that every participant must vote; and voting without durably recording the intended change would leave nothing to recover from if a crash happened mid-transaction.\n\nSo the answer is: Phase 1 has each Resource Manager durably record its intended change and vote, without applying it yet; Phase 2 only then has each one actually apply the change, once every vote is yes."
