      "modelAnswer": "It's like a group booking where everyone has to say yes before the deposit is paid — if one person never replies, the organiser can't just go ahead on the others' yeses, and the booking stalls until that reply is sorted out, even though nothing has actually gone wrong with anyone's money yet.\n\n• What the Transaction Manager should do: abort the whole admission, and tell Bed-Management — which already voted yes — to roll back its prepared bed allocation, since a 2PC transaction only ever commits once every participant has voted yes, and Billing never got the chance to vote.\n\n• Why this is safe: Phase 1 never actually applied any real change, so rolling back Bed-Management's merely-prepared allocation doesn't undo anything that was ever visible to the rest of the system.\n\n• Why it's an availability risk, not a consistency risk: the real cost here is that the whole admission is now blocked or delayed, possibly for a long time, while the Transaction Manager waits to find out whether Billing is reachable again — 2PC is sometimes described as an \"unavailability protocol\" for exactly this reason, since a single unresponsive participant can stall the whole transaction even though the eventual outcome stays fully consistent.\n\n• A common mistake is: treating a crash as an automatic yes risks genuine inconsistency between the two systems, which is exactly what 2PC is designed to prevent; retrying indefinitely without ever aborting ignores that the whole point of 2PC is to reach a decision; and letting one system commit independently of the other defeats the entire purpose of coordinating them together.\n\nSo the answer is: abort and roll back Bed-Management's prepared allocation — this is an availability risk because the admission is blocked waiting on Billing, not a consistency risk, since nothing was ever actually applied."
