      "modelAnswer": "It's like keeping a receipt of exactly what you promised to do, so that even if you get interrupted halfway through doing it, you can check the receipt afterward and finish the job correctly instead of having to ask everyone involved what you were even supposed to do.\n\n• What the Resource Manager does on restart: it consults its own durable transaction log — written before or as part of applying the commit instruction — and sees from that log that this transaction was told to commit.\n\n• What it does next: it can safely re-apply, or confirm it already applied, the bed allocation, and report completion back to the Transaction Manager, without needing to re-ask what to do.\n\n• Why the log makes this possible: because each Resource Manager (and the Transaction Manager) independently keeps its own durable log, a crash at any point can be recovered from using that log, instead of having to re-run the whole prepare/vote exchange from scratch.\n\n• A common mistake is: a crashed Resource Manager isn't left needing a manual, from-scratch fix; it doesn't need to restart the prepare/vote exchange, since the commit decision is already known and logged; and this recovery mechanism is a deliberate part of how 2PC participants are built, not something PostgreSQL's own generic crash recovery happens to provide on its own.\n\nSo the answer is: the Resource Manager recovers by reading its own durable transaction log, which already recorded the commit decision, letting it safely re-apply or confirm the change without re-asking the Transaction Manager."
