      "modelAnswer": "Availability is just the fraction of time something is actually working, so everything needs to be measured in the same units before comparing or combining figures — like making sure you're not adding hours to days by mistake.\n\n• Converting to hours: Bedside Sensor Gateway MTTF = 45 days = 1,080 hours, MTTR = 3 hours. Alert Processing Server MTTF = 90 days = 2,160 hours, MTTR = 30 minutes = 0.5 hours. Nurse Notification Service MTTF = 60 days = 1,440 hours, MTTR = 1 hour.\n\n• The formula: A = MTTF / (MTTF + MTTR) — the fraction of total time (working time plus repair time) that was actually spent working.\n\n• The results: Gateway ≈ 1,080/1,083 ≈ 99.723%. Alert Server ≈ 2,160/2,160.5 ≈ 99.977%. Notification Service ≈ 1,440/1,441 ≈ 99.931%.\n\n• A common mistake is: leaving units mixed (some in days, some in hours) without converting first distorts the fractions badly; using MTTF/MTTR instead of MTTF/(MTTF+MTTR) produces numbers that aren't even valid percentages; and swapping the Gateway's and Alert Server's correctly-calculated figures just assigns the right numbers to the wrong components.\n\nSo the answer is: Gateway ≈ 99.723%, Alert Server ≈ 99.977%, Notification Service ≈ 99.931%, found using A = MTTF/(MTTF+MTTR) with everything converted to hours first."
