      "modelAnswer": "Reversible is like glancing at a shelf — looking costs nothing and changes nothing. Compensable is like moving an item to a different shelf — easy to put back if it turns out to be the wrong spot. Irreversible is like shouting a warning across a crowded room — once it's out, you can't call it back, even if it turns out to have been a false alarm.\n\n• Looking up a patient's history: Reversible — a read-only call with no side effect at all, so the agent can simply be allowed to do it without needing approval first.\n\n• Creating a priority flag: Compensable — it changes state, but that state (the flag) can be cleared or corrected afterward if the escalation turns out to have been wrong.\n\n• Paging the on-call doctor: Irreversible, or very close to it — once a real person has been paged and alerted, that notification can't be \"un-sent\", and the real-world interruption (and any resulting alert fatigue) has already happened even if the page turns out to have been unnecessary.\n\n• The design rule: classify every tool by reversibility times blast radius — never by how \"confident\" the model's output appears to be — and require human approval, or withhold the tool entirely, for anything irreversible with meaningful blast radius, such as paging the on-call doctor.\n\n• A common mistake is: swapping history-lookup and paging gets their risk completely backwards; treating every action as equally reversible ignores that a page genuinely can't be undone; and basing the design rule on cost, or claiming EHR flags can never be edited, misses the actual factor that matters — what real-world effect the action has and how hard it is to take back.\n\nSo the answer is: history lookup is reversible, the priority flag is compensable, and paging the doctor is irreversible — classify by reversibility times blast radius, not by model confidence."
